---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { site } from '../config';
---
<Base title="Contact GuideHollow" description="Report a broken link, suggest an article correction, verify editorial evidence, or reach GuideHollow privately by email or publicly on GitHub." breadcrumbs={[{name:'Contact',url:'/contact/'}]}>
<header class="page-heading"><p class="eyebrow mint">/ contact</p><h1>Help make the path clearer.</h1><p>Found an outdated software step, broken link, calculation discrepancy, or topic gap our guides should cover? Reach the maintainer directly below.</p></header><article class="prose info-content">{site.contactEmail && <section><h2>Private correspondence</h2><p>For questions you prefer not to post on a public tracker—such as privacy requests, copyright notices, or editorial feedback—write directly to <a href={`mailto:${site.contactEmail}`}>{site.contactEmail}</a>. Your chosen email provider handles message delivery. Share only the details needed for your request and never send passwords, API keys, or confidential customer records.</p></section>}<h2>Public correction tracker</h2><p>For reproducible technical issues, broken external citations, accessibility findings, or fixture defects, our public issue tracker on GitHub lets any reader inspect reported problems and verify when fixes ship. Signing into GitHub is required only to open or comment on an issue; all existing reports and fix commits remain publicly readable without signing in.</p><p><a class="button primary" href={site.contact}>Open a GitHub issue ↗</a></p><p><a href={`${site.repository}/issues`}>View existing issues</a></p><h2>Choose the right contact channel</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Reason for contacting</th><th>Recommended channel</th><th>What to include</th><th>How fixes are published</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Outdated vendor documentation or UI label</td><td>GitHub issue or private email</td><td>Exact GuideHollow page URL plus the current official product Help Center link</td><td>Article updated with revised <code>updatedDate</code> and public commit note</td></tr><tr><td>Tool bug or CSV/TSV fixture question</td><td>GitHub issue</td><td>Browser version, steps to reproduce, and non-sensitive sample rows</td><td>Regression test added alongside the code or fixture fix</td></tr><tr><td>Broken download, image, or internal link</td><td>GitHub issue</td><td>Page URL and failing route or asset filename</td><td>Fixed and checked across automated build and readiness gates</td></tr><tr><td>Privacy, takedown, or private inquiry</td><td><a href={`mailto:${site.contactEmail}`}>Direct email</a></td><td>Relevant URL and summary of your request without sensitive secrets</td><td>Handled privately; <a href="/privacy-policy/">Privacy Policy</a> updated if practices change</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>What helps us verify a correction quickly</h2><p>Every factual update on GuideHollow must trace back to an observable check or an official source rather than hearsay. Including these four details lets us verify and resolve a report cleanly:</p><ul><li>The exact GuideHollow URL and article or tool title.</li><li>The specific paragraph, table row, diagram, or tool behavior that needs attention.</li><li>What you expected versus what you observed (for example, a changed vendor setting name or a broken download path).</li><li>An official reference link (such as vendor Help Center documentation or W3C specification) when proposing a factual correction.</li></ul><h2>How corrections and reader feedback are handled</h2><p>When a reader submits a documented error or usability issue, we reproduce the scenario against our published inputs and check the cited official source before editing the page. Material factual fixes update the visible guide revision date and record the change in the repository history so readers know what changed and why. Minor typo fixes or visual spacing improvements do not rewrite publication dates.</p><p>Before reporting a mismatch in our downloadable datasets—such as the <a href="/evidence/">Evidence Library</a> CSV, TSV, PDF, or XLSX exercises—please review the accompanying walkthrough and scope notes, as several examples intentionally include edge cases (such as zero-price rows, midnight timestamps, or raster-only PDFs) to illustrate how verification catches subtle errors.</p><h2>Topics we cannot help with</h2><ul><li>Recovering lost third-party AI accounts, resetting chat passwords, or bypassing workspace permissions: contact your AI vendor's official support team directly.</li><li>Reviewing private medical, legal, tax, or confidential client contracts: GuideHollow provides general educational workflows only and does not offer personalized professional advice or consulting.</li><li>Paid link insertions, sponsored rankings, or guest-post exchanges: GuideHollow rejects paid editorial placements to keep guides independent.</li></ul><h2>Keep personal information out of public issues</h2><p>Public GitHub issues are indexed and readable by anyone on the internet. Never paste API credentials, secret tokens, patient records, unreleased company budgets, or private customer transcripts into an issue report. If an example involves a secret exposure scenario, replace credentials with clearly synthetic placeholders.</p><p>{site.contactEmail ? 'There is no web contact form on this site; use direct email above whenever your message should stay private.' : 'We do not currently publish a private email contact or operate a contact form. If you need private correspondence, do not submit confidential information to the public tracker.'} We review incoming private email messages and reproducible technical reports regularly and aim to respond within 2 to 4 business days.</p></article>
</Base>
